name mig29-opt-low-1
timeout 120

# THE DIRECTOR DELIVERY, and the mission that closes campaign gap C9. This is the FIRST air-to-ground
# release by a MiG-29 in this tree, and it is deliberately NOT the F-16's procedure with Cyrillic
# labels: the pilot does not pick the release moment, because on this aircraft the AIRCRAFT does
# (doc/modules/mig29/weapons.md §5.4).
#
# TWO UNITS, ONE GEOMETRY, TWO PROCEDURES — and that pairing IS the mission. The MiG flies its own OPT
# director; the F-16 flies its CCRP release cue. Same altitude, same speed, same run-in length, same
# store (fab500 on both, so the ballistics are not a variable), separate targets 6.7 km apart so
# neither sees the other. A single number would prove nothing; the DIFFERENCE is the measurement.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS ON THE MiG, in the order events.log prints it:
#   pilot ATTACK_LOCKON      the rangefinder is fired when the aiming mark is ~8 s short of the target.
#                            The pilot converts the mark's DISTANCE into a time himself: before the
#                            trigger this computer publishes a range scale and no countdown at all
#   director LRF_RANGE       the slant range, inside the device's documented 6 km reach
#   pilot ATTACK_CONSENT     the trigger, pressed 2.5 s later — INSIDE the documented 1.5...4 s
#                            accuracy window and never before the documented 1 s minimum. It is a
#                            CONSENT: nothing leaves the jet on this press
#   director DIRECTOR_ARM    the plan — a release time, a commanded normal load factor (the "ring of
#                            the specified overload") and the flight path and speed it was frozen from.
#                            Computed ONCE, never re-solved
#   sms RELEASE              the AIRCRAFT lets go, at zero on its own countdown
#   director DIRECTOR_RELEASE  what the plan cost: meanGErr/maxBankDeg say how well the pilot flew the
#                            ring, and openLoopAlongM is the PROCEDURE'S OWN ERROR — the same stored
#                            table run twice, once as the plan predicted and once from the state the
#                            aircraft actually had. It contains no table error at all, and it is
#                            exactly zero for a computer that re-solves
#   stores DELIVERY          predErrM (table against the store's own model) and aimErrM (what arrived)
#
# WHY THE FINAL IS ONLY 6 KM, and it is not a convenience. This airframe does not hold a straight leg:
# its yaw channel is measured OFF (doc/modules/mig29/module.md gap 4i) and it acquires a standing
# cross-track offset that saturates at ~90 m within the first 10 km of any leg. Measured on this exact
# geometry stretched to 12 km: MiG aimAcrossM 92.2 m against the F-16's 8.6 m on the identical line —
# a factor of 11 that has nothing to do with the delivery procedure and everything to do with the
# airframe. A 6 km final keeps it near 45 m, inside the FAB-500's lethal radius against a soft
# position, and it happens to be the doctrinally right answer anyway: nobody flies twelve straight
# kilometres at 300 m into a defended target.
#
# WHY 300 M. The release point sits one bomb-throw short of the target, the designation has to happen
# ~8 s before that, and the rangefinder only reaches 6 km. Those three numbers together cap this
# aircraft's level-bombing altitude — see mig29-opt-refused.fbm, where the same run REFUSES. O3's own
# anchor sentence ("came in very low") therefore falls out of one documented number.
#
# RUN IT WITH `--elev const`, for the reason attack-ccrp.fbm gives: with no `runway` line that provider
# is a flat 0 m base, so the computer's impact plane, the target's elevation and the ground the physics
# judge resolves against are the same number and predErrM is purely the computer's own error.
#
# READING RULE: exit 0 = SUCCESS, and it needs BOTH positions down — `objective kill unit` on each
# striker, so a run in which only the F-16 delivers is a FAIL and says so. The MEASUREMENT is the two
# `stores DELIVERY` lines side by side. A director is the less accurate procedure; if the MiG's
# aimErrM ever comes out BELOW the F-16's here, the director has been built as a release cue and the
# run is a false pass.
# MEASURED (2026-07-30, --elev const):
#   fulcrum (OPT director)  countdown 4.10 s, cmdG 1.000, meanGErr 0.052, maxBankDeg 0.88
#                           solAgeS 4.1 s   openLoopAlongM -69.3 m
#                           aimErrM 65.65 m (long -47.88, across 44.91)
#   viper   (CCRP cue)      solAgeS 0.1 s   openLoop 0 by construction
#                           aimErrM 34.02 m (long  33.88, across  3.13)
#   -> the director is 1.93x the cue's total error, and the two impacts sit 82 m apart ALONG the track
#      on the identical geometry with the identical store. The whole 82 m is the frozen plan: the same
#      table integrated from the plan's assumed release state and from the real one differ by 69.3 m
#      (openLoopAlongM), which is a quantity a re-solving computer has equal to zero. The breakdown, and
#      what it does NOT prove, is in doc/modules/mig29/weapons.md §5.4.4.

unit fulcrum
  module mig29
  team hostile
  spawn 46.90000 6.97800  300 90.0 450       # due east at the target, co-track, 6 km out, low
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set n019_emission off                       # nothing here is an air engagement, and on this jet
                                              # radiating is the one thing a striker must not do
  set store 3 fab500                          # left inboard pylon
  set store 6 fab500                          # its mirror — never dropped: one pass, one store
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode opt                         # the ONLY value this airframe accepts
  wp 46.90000 7.05000 300 450                 # THE TARGET: the active steerpoint IS the aim point
  wp 46.95000 7.20000 2000 400                # the egress point, flown after the break-off turn
  objective kill unit battery

unit battery
  module target_soft                          # an artillery position: unprotected, no FDM
  team friendly
  spawn 46.90000 7.05000 ground 90.0 0

# THE CONTROL. Everything a striker can share with the MiG is shared: altitude, speed, run-in length,
# store, target class. What differs is the ONE thing under test — the delivery procedure.
unit viper
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.84000 6.97800  300 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off
  set store 3 fab500
  set store 7 fab500
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode ccrp
  wp 46.84000 7.05000 300 450
  wp 46.79000 7.20000 2000 400
  objective kill unit bunker

unit bunker
  module target_soft
  team hostile
  spawn 46.84000 7.05000 ground 90.0 0
